## The Capability Gap We Wanted to Train

A lot of current AI benchmarks are too clean.

They often assume:
- a single objective
- immediate feedback
- stable conditions
- no real human variability
- no meaningful long-term consequences

But real systems are messy.

In EnergyMind:
- rooms have different occupants
- some rooms are higher priority than others
- occupancy changes over time
- complaints build up when users are repeatedly underserved
- total power is limited
- crisis conditions like heatwaves increase pressure on the system

This means the agent cannot simply maximize one number blindly. Serving one room may hurt another. Reducing energy use too aggressively may create complaint cascades later. Ignoring high-priority users may look efficient in the short term but fail badly over an episode.

This is why EnergyMind fits **Theme #1: Multi-Agent Interactions** so strongly. One system must manage multiple human actors with different incentives and needs. It also fits **Theme #2: Long-Horizon Planning**, because mistakes do not disappear. They accumulate.

