This mattered for one reason: it showed that EnergyMind is not just reward shaping theater. The environment produces a real signal that learned policies can improve on.

That gave us confidence that this is a legitimate training environment, not just a story.

Closed-Loop Post-Training Pipeline
Once the environment and executor were stable, we used the environment itself to drive LLM post-training.

Our pipeline supports both offline and online-style learning.

1. Reward-derived strategy preferences
For each environment state, we evaluate available high-level strategies and rank them by actual environment reward. From that, we build preference pairs of:

a better strategy
a worse strategy
These pairs are saved as:

data/llm_strategy_preferences.jsonl
2. Offline preference distillation
We then train the LLM planner using these reward-derived preferences so that it learns to choose better governance strategies.

3. Grouped online-style rollouts
We also collect grouped rollouts that record:

sampled strategy
parsed output validity
reward
complaints
high-priority satisfaction
normalized advantage
These rollouts are saved as:

data/llm_strategy_grpo_rollouts.jsonl

4. GRPO-lite updates
Finally, we run an online-style reward-weighted update loop over rollout data.

This means EnergyMind is not just an evaluation benchmark. It is a real closed-loop post-training environment:

model acts
environment returns reward
reward becomes learning signal
model updates
behavior improves
